  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 302 PM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nSynoptically, starting Thursday morning, an upper-level ridge \nis forecast to be positioned along the Southern US / Gulf \nborder. Ensemble guidance and deterministic models place the \nridge axis along the Missouri River Valley region, establishing \nsouthwesterly upper level flow across the Tri-State Area. In \naddition to the ridge to the south, both the GFS and Canadian-NH\nbring a shortwave through early Thursday morning, accompanied \nby an associated vorticity maximum, and jet streak. Additional \nshortwaves may move through the region during the latter part of\nthe period as the ridge begins to shift westward.\n\nHigh temperatures for the week are forecast to mainly in the \n70s. OVer the weekend, expect temperatures to briefly warm into \nthe low to mid 80s across the majority of the CWA. Sustained \nwinds and gusts are forecast to stay under 25 mph, prevailing \nmainly out of the south to southeast. Friday looks to be the \nwindiest, with gusts up to 20 mph. \n\nPrecipitation chances will be higher late in the week before \ntapering off over the weekend. As the shortwave move through \nearly Thursday morning, Probabilities of Precipitation (PoPs) \nare currently forecast at 60-80% for most of the Tri-State area \nas a surface low with an associated boundary/front passes \nthrough. For Friday, guidance hints at a surface low developing \nout of Colorado; however, model agreement varies, with the ECMWF\nand GFS showing the low while the Canadian and ensemble \nmembers, like the LREF, do not. Where this low develops and \ntracks will impact Friday and potentially Saturday morning PoPs.\nFriday's PoPs are forecast in the 20-50% range, potentially \nending early Saturday morning. Saturday afternoon through Monday\nPoPs remain around 15-30% each afternoon.",
